{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Drink no wine nor strong drink.</b> — <b>Wine</b> through its intoxicating quality — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 10:9). And its meaning is that if he diluted it or put a little water into it, he is exempt. And the opinion of the Rav is that they were warned only about wine, not about other intoxicants. And <b>sheikhar</b> is learned by analogy from the Nazirite (Nazir 4a). And so too is the matter according to my opinion. But according to the opinion of the Rav, Rabbi Moshe (Sefer haMitzvot, Negative Commandment 73; and Hilkhot Bi'at haMikdash 1:2), [the prohibition applies] to other intoxicants as a warning, and <b>sheikhar</b> according to its plain sense. And the reason for this commandment now is that the priest not err from the intoxication of wine and come to an improper thought and die on account of it, as his sons did. And it is possible that what the Sages expounded (Leviticus Rabbah 12:1) — that Nadav and Avihu had drunk wine — is to say that because of their wine they erred regarding strange fire. Not that the punishment was because of the wine, for they had not yet been warned against it, but their punishment was that they erred regarding the fire of YHWH, as I have already hinted (Ramban on Leviticus 10:2)." "<b>When you come into the Tent of Meeting.</b> — \"I have only [the law] regarding their coming into the Temple; from where [do I learn the law regarding] their approaching the altar? It says here 'entering the Tent,' and it says regarding sanctification of hands and feet 'entering the Tent' (Exodus 30:20). Just as there, approaching the altar is treated as entering the Tent, so too here, approaching the altar is treated as entering the Tent.\" This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 10:9) and of Torat Kohanim (Sifra, parashah 1:4). And it appears, according to this comparison, that the priest is not liable to death for approaching the altar or for entering the Tent unless he performed an act of divine service there, just as with regard to sanctification of hands and feet, the warning there is only that he not perform divine service, as it says (Exodus 30:20), \"or in their approaching the altar to minister, to burn an offering made by fire to YHWH.\" And in this language it is stated there in the Sifra: Rabbi says, it says here \"when you come into the Tent of Meeting,\" and it says there (Exodus 30:20) \"when they come into the Tent of Meeting\" — just as there, Scripture made the exit like the entrance and the altar like the Tent of Meeting, and he is liable only at the time of divine service, so too here, Scripture made the exit like the entrance and the altar like the Tent of Meeting, and he is liable only at the time of divine service. That is to say, the liability is only that he not perform divine service, whether he entered intoxicated or drank there and then performed divine service. And there (Sifra 1:8) they said: \"An everlasting statute\" — to include the procedures of arranging, mixing, waving, presenting, handful-gathering, burning, hand-pressing, and sprinkling. For since Scripture said \"to burn an offering made by fire to YHWH\" (Exodus 30:20), these were needed to include them. And it further appears that this liability does not apply to one who offers on a bamah, and therefore they said there: \"I have only the Tent of Meeting; from where to include Shiloh and the eternal House? Scripture says 'an everlasting statute.'\" For the warning to the priests applies to their divine service, and there is no priest at a bamah. And behold, the meaning of \"when you come\" is: when you perform divine service, and it is stated in this language because all the divine services there were either inside the Tent or on the altar of burnt offering that is at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting. And the general principle is that the warning and the punishment regarding these are only that he not perform divine service [while] intoxicated with wine or while unwashed of hands and feet, and likewise lacking the [priestly] garments. But regarding entry, there is no prohibition in the Torah for any of these. And what we learned (Kelim 1:9) — \"Rabbi Yosei says: In five respects the area between the porch and the altar is equivalent to the Temple: those with blemishes, those with disheveled hair, and those who have not washed hands and feet do not enter there\" — all of these are Rabbinic degrees of sanctity. And according to the opinion of the Rav, Rabbi Moshe (Hilkhot Bi'at haMikdash 1:15–16), there is a negative commandment for entry without divine service, but it does not carry the penalty of death."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "I rendered ר' משה as 'the Rav, Rabbi Moshe' following Ramban's practice of first referring to Maimonides by the title haRav and then specifying the name; subsequent occurrences in the same comment use 'the Rav, Rabbi Moshe' as well.", 1 "The phrase מעלות דרבנן (ma'alot derabbanan) is rendered literally as 'Rabbinic degrees of sanctity'; it refers to gradations of sanctity enacted by Rabbinic decree."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:39.859027566Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}